  <article class="almanac-prose">
    <section id="why-one" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02.1 · one stable tool</span>
        <h2>One tool keeps prompts small.</h2>
        <p>Many agent systems add one function definition for every action. Dekopon always exposes the same function: <code>bash</code> with one <code>script</code> string. That stays true whether you grant two capabilities or two hundred.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="authority-state-machine">
        <div><span>model receives</span><strong><code>bash({ script })</code></strong><small>one fixed schema</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">writes a plan →</i>
        <div class="authority-broker-box">
          <span>inside the sandboxed interpreter</span>
          <div><strong><code>cap --list</code></strong><b>→</b><strong>loops + jq</strong><b>→</b><strong>capability calls</strong></div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→ limited result</i>
        <div><span>model receives</span><strong>output + exit code</strong><small>replayable by an operator</small></div>
      </div>

      <p>One tool call can discover available actions, fetch several values, filter the results, make a choice, and call another capability. That saves model round trips without turning the agent into a local shell user.</p>

      <div class="article-callout callout-decision">
        <strong>The name “bash” describes the syntax, not the access.</strong>
        <p><code>dekopon-shell</code> is a Rust interpreter. It never launches <code>/bin/bash</code>, starts a process, opens a file, reads your environment, or creates a socket.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="data-path" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02.2 · structured data</span>
        <h2>JSON stays JSON.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="data-journey-map">
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-remote">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span>untrusted model output</span><strong>one tool call</strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node"><small>model protocol</small><strong><code>{"script":"..."}</code></strong><code>JSON object required</code></div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↓ check the tool name and script</i>
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-host">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span><code>dekopon-run</code></span><strong>pure Rust interpreter</strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node-row">
            <div class="journey-node"><small>parser</small><strong>script structure</strong><code>unsupported syntax fails clearly</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→ run →</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>data</small><strong><code>serde_json::Value</code></strong><code>arrays + maps + simple values</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→ call →</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>tool gate</small><strong>local or broker</strong><code>same result shape</code></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↓ structured input · limited structured output</i>
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-guest">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span>fresh sandbox</span><strong>Wasm provider or broker request</strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node-row">
            <div class="journey-node"><small>input</small><strong>JSON object</strong><code>provider checks its fields</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→ run →</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>result</small><strong>succeeded / failed / denied</strong><code>keeps its meaning</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→ return →</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>pipeline</small><strong>structured value</strong><code>no extra text parsing</code></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>Pipelines pass JSON values instead of raw bytes, much like <code>jq</code>. Command substitution can preserve a whole structured value. A flag such as <code>--post-id 7</code> becomes <code>{"postId":7}</code>, and repeated flags become arrays. Providers do not have to parse custom text between every step.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="language" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02.3 · a useful subset</span>
        <h2>Keep useful shell ideas. Leave the OS behind.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Available</th><th scope="col">How it behaves</th><th scope="col">What stays absent</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Control flow</th><td><code>if</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>while</code>, <code>until</code>, <code>case</code>, functions, break/continue</td><td>subshells, background jobs, process substitution</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Structured work</th><td>real <code>jq</code>, JSON variables, arrays/maps, arithmetic, structured pipelines</td><td><code>jq env</code>, host clock filters, inherited variables</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Text utilities</th><td><code>grep</code>, <code>sed</code>, <code>cut</code>, <code>sort</code>, <code>uniq</code>, <code>wc</code>, base64, <code>xargs</code></td><td>unbounded regex/glob behavior or silently ignored flags</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Named buffers</th><td><code>&gt;</code>/<code>&gt;&gt;</code> write in-memory values read only by <code>cat</code></td><td>filesystem paths, descriptors, devices, pipes</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Capability access</th><td>bare capability names, <code>cap</code>, optionally capability-backed <code>curl</code></td><td>raw sockets, DNS, subprocess commands, arbitrary host calls</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Clock</th><td><code>date</code> only after explicit <code>--shell-allow-clock</code>, UTC only</td><td>clock access by default, timezone environment, setting the clock</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Dangerous or unsupported syntax fails clearly. Dekopon does not pretend to run <code>eval</code>, <code>exec</code>, <code>source</code>, backticks, file-descriptor redirects, or unsupported array and pattern features. Wildcards stay plain characters because there is no filesystem to search.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="dispatch" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02.4 · one tool gate</span>
        <h2>Every outside action goes through one gate.</h2>
      </div>

      <pre><code><span class="code-purple">pub trait</span> CapabilityInvoker {
    <span class="code-purple">fn</span> granted(&amp;self) -&gt; Vec&lt;String&gt;;
    <span class="code-purple">fn</span> describe(&amp;self, capability: &amp;str) -&gt; Option&lt;CapabilityDescription&gt;;
    <span class="code-purple">fn</span> invoke(
        &amp;self,
        capability: &amp;str,
        input: serde_json::Value,
    ) -&gt; CapabilityCallResult;
}</code></pre>

      <div class="future-topology">
        <article>
          <div><span>FIRST LEG</span>{{ status("current", "Direct") }}</div>
          <h3>Pure local tools</h3>
          <p>If a pure capability is loaded locally, the runner starts it in a fresh Wasm sandbox. It does not get network permission or a broker audit record.</p>
          <strong>Good for</strong><ul><li>pure transforms</li><li>structured computation</li><li>read-only capabilities</li></ul>
        </article>
        <div class="topology-crossing"><span>when the tool needs broker access</span><strong>request →</strong><small>no identity claim</small></div>
        <article>
          <div><span>SECOND LEG</span>{{ status("current", "Broker") }}</div>
          <h3>Permissioned network tools</h3>
          <p>With <code>prompt --broker</code>, tools that are not local go to the separate broker. A denied request returns exit code <code>126</code> instead of looking like a provider bug.</p>
          <strong>Good for</strong><ul><li>limited HTTP</li><li>separate read and write actions</li><li>safe evidence + durable audit</li></ul>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p>The built-in <code>curl</code> command follows the same rule. It turns familiar flags into <code>{uri, method, headers, body}</code> and sends them to one capability you selected. A pure local tool cannot load an HTTP interface. A networked tool still needs broker approval for the request.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="budgets" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02.5 · hard limits</span>
        <h2>Different risks get different limits.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="invariant-notes">
        <article><span>script</span><h3>Plan limits</h3><p>Steps, nesting, recursion, data size, output size, wall time, and capability calls.</p></article>
        <article><span>session</span><h3>Session limits</h3><p>At most four tool calls per model turn, with one capability-call budget shared across every script in the prompt session.</p></article>
        <article><span>Wasm</span><h3>Provider limits</h3><p>A fresh Store limits memory, fuel, input, output, and time for every provider operation.</p></article>
        <article><span>broker</span><h3>I/O limits</h3><p>Concurrency, message size, time, network calls, HTTP bytes, destination, method, and provider output.</p></article>
      </div>

      <p>Separate limits matter because different workloads fail in different ways. A loop uses steps. Repeated tools use calls. Growing a string uses memory. A fast script can still call a slow provider. Each layer limits the resource it owns.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="telemetry" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02.6 · safe tracing</span>
        <h2>Trace the command, not its secrets.</h2>
      </div>

      <p>Every executed command becomes a <code>shell.command</code> span under <code>prompt.script</code>. The span keeps a safe built-in name, command type, argument <em>count</em>, exit code, and result. Custom function names and unknown words become <code>&lt;withheld&gt;</code>. Argument values never become trace fields.</p>

      <div class="article-callout callout-plain">
        <strong>Why count arguments instead of recording them?</strong>
        <p><code>curl -d '{"apiKey":"..."}'</code> and <code>cap some.id '{"token":"..."}'</code> can carry sensitive provider input. Recording every argument would create a new path for that data to leave the sandbox.</p>
      </div>

